<GuidePage title='Designing for the Unknown - App Router Guide' root='..'>
  <div class='kicker'>Chapter 12 · Part B: Contain the Unknown</div>
  <h1>Designing for the Unknown</h1>
  <p class='lede'>
    Everything in <a href='10-containers-content-shifting.html'>§10</a> and
    <a href='11-suspense-streaming.html'>§11</a> assumed the layout <em>can</em> be contained. That is
    not an engineering given; it is decided earlier, in design. Some layouts are containable and some
    fight containment no matter how carefully they are implemented, which makes stability a cross-team
    goal: engineering, UX, and design deciding together what the page does while the data is still unknown.
  </p>

  <Callout kind='voice' title='"Design is how it works"'>
    <p>
      "Most people make the mistake of thinking design is what it looks like. People think it's this
      veneer, that the designers are handed this box and told, 'Make it look good!' That's not what
      we think design is. It's not just what it looks like and feels like. Design is how it works."
      <br />
      <span style='color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.92em'
        >Steve Jobs, in The New York Times Magazine, 2003</span
      >
    </p>
    <p>
      That cuts both ways. If design is how it works, then design has technical elements, always,
      and the people who know how it works are obligated to be part of designing it. Comps aren't
      scripture to implement and mocks aren't the end of the conversation: how a layout behaves
      while its data is unknown is a design property, and it's one that engineering understands
      first. This chapter is about carrying that knowledge back across the table.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id='shared-goal'>12.1 Stability is a cross-team goal</h2>
  <p>
    Cumulative Layout Shift is the engineering measurement of a goal that is fundamentally shared: <strong
      >the page must not move under the user.</strong
    > Nothing they are reading jumps, nothing they are about to click dodges, nothing "settles" in three
    visible steps. The metric lives in engineering's world, but its causes mostly do not: they live in
    composition decisions (what shares an axis with what, what gets how much space, what the page shows
    before the data) that are made in design, before any code exists. A goal whose measurement belongs
    to one team and whose causes belong to another cannot be owned by either alone. It has to be a standing
    conversation.
  </p>
  <p>
    Today that conversation mostly doesn't happen, and the reason it must is timing.
    <Chip k='very' /> By the time engineering discovers a layout is unstable, the design is approved,
    the component is half built, and every fix is a compromise: a hardcoded height that is wrong half
    the time, a spinner where a skeleton should be, a container that scrolls awkwardly because the space
    it was given never matched its content. The cheap moment to catch instability is
    <em>while the design is still a design</em>: identifying the places where a composition
    introduces content that is hard to contain, early enough that the UX can be shaped toward
    predictability instead of patched toward it. Done consistently, that pressure compounds: the
    design system itself drifts toward layouts that are stable by default, for the screens being
    built now and the ones not yet designed.
  </p>

  <Callout kind='voice' title='What the channel is for'>
    <p>
      Not a review gate, a two-way channel. Engineering brings what only engineering can see: which
      regions arrive late, in what order, and what the page does in the states the mock doesn't
      show. UX brings what only UX can see: which compositions are worth the containment cost and
      where stability should bend to hierarchy. The failure mode this chapter exists to prevent is
      both sides discovering the conflict after the layout is built.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id='happy-path'>12.2 The comp is a happy path</h2>
  <p>
    A Figma comp is a snapshot of exactly one state: the <strong>happy path</strong>. The data has
    arrived, every region is populated, and the row counts were chosen so the composition looks
    balanced inside the frame: the table shows the six rows that fit, the cards have names of
    pleasant length, and nothing is empty, loading, or overflowing. None of this is laziness; it is
    what comps are <em>for</em>: selling a composition. But a static tool produces static answers,
    and the states that break layouts are precisely the ones a static frame never has to confront:
    the second before the data arrives, the day the table has one row, the customer whose program
    list needs three screens of scrolling.
  </p>
  <p>
    The subtler version of the problem survives even in thorough comps. Often the empty state <em
      >is</em
    > mocked, but it's drawn at the same size the container had when it held the artboard's eight rows,
    as if "empty" were just different content in the same box. The box only had that size because eight
    rows fit it; the height was an artifact of the sample data, not a decision. What the comp never shows
    is the page
    <em>flipping between</em> those states: empty today, 1 row after the first save, 300 rows next quarter,
    with everything below the container living or dying by which state it's in. The count is never knowable
    ahead of time; that is permanent. So the real design question is not "what does empty look like?"
    but
    <strong
      >"how do all of these states swap through the same space without pushing everything else
      around?"</strong
    >
  </p>
  <p>
    So the questions engineering must put on the table, for every data-driven region in every mock,
    are these:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>"What does this region show before its content is known?"</strong> Not "a spinner": what
      shape, what height, what happens to its neighbors while it waits (<a
        href='11-suspense-streaming.html#fallback-trade'>§11.2</a
      >).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>"What happens when the content is much smaller than this?"</strong> One row. Zero rows:
      the empty state is a designed state, not a gap (<a
        href='11-suspense-streaming.html#orthogonal'>§11.6</a
      >).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>"What happens when the content is much larger than this?"</strong> Does the region grow
      (pushing what's below it), scroll, paginate, or truncate? Someone must pick one, and the pick changes
      the layout.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>"Which of these regions arrive at different times?"</strong> Two regions that stream independently
      (<a href='11-suspense-streaming.html'>§11</a>) will resolve in either order; the design has to
      look intentional in both.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    These questions are the channel from <a href='#shared-goal'>§12.1</a> in practice, and they flow both
    ways. The more consistently they get asked, the more natural they become on both sides: engineers
    start noticing containment problems in mocks reflexively and raising them while the design is still
    cheap to change, and UX starts anticipating the unknown-size states in the compositions themselves,
    before anyone has to ask. The deliverable is not any single fixed mock; it is that both teams end
    up thinking in states by default.
  </p>

  <h2 id='stacked-tables'>12.3 The worked example: two stacked tables</h2>
  <p>
    Here is a common hard case, and it looks completely innocent in a comp: two tables stacked
    vertically inside the app chrome, say <em>Assignments</em> above
    <em>Submissions</em> on a program page. In the mock, each shows a handful of rows and together they
    fill the content area exactly. Now walk the states. On first paint the row counts are unknown (and
    even with SSR, the <em>server</em> knows them but the
    <em>designer</em> still didn't when the heights were drawn). So how much vertical space does each
    table get?
  </p>
  <p>
    Every naive answer fails a different way. <strong>Guess 1, split the height 50/50:</strong> the guess
    is wrong almost every time, because real data is skewed: the top table has 1 assignment and sits in
    a sea of dead space while the bottom one crams 50 submissions into the same half and scrolls furiously.
    <strong>Guess 2, size each table to its content with a min-height:</strong> now the pre-data state
    reserves the min-height, but the moment the top table's 30 rows arrive it grows and
    <em>shoves the bottom table down the page</em>, possibly below the fold: the exact
    reading-interrupting shift this whole effort exists to prevent, and it happens on every load, in
    front of the user. <strong>Guess 3, just let both tables be auto-height:</strong> no constraints at
    all, so the table bodies are never contained and never scroll; the content area around them (usually
    the whole page) becomes the scroll container instead. The shift is the same as Guess 2: whatever the
    top table turns out to be pushes everything below it, except now the page's total length is also data-dependent,
    the scrollbar jumps as each table resolves, and the app chrome is the only thing standing still. Auto-height
    is not a neutral default; it is a decision to let the data lay out the page.
  </p>

