  <p>
    All three resolutions share the property that matters:
    <strong
      >the space each region occupies is decided by design, before the data, instead of by whichever
      response happens to arrive.</strong
    > That is what "contain the unknown" means at the design layer.
  </p>
  <p>
    And be clear about what the partitioned version is: <em>a compromise, not the ideal</em>. It
    makes the stacked composition survivable (nothing moves), but the user still pays for it: a pane
    that is mostly dead space when its table is sparse, an internal scrollbar working hard when it
    is busy, two regions forever negotiating one axis. The genuinely better outcome is the one
    further upstream, where the composition never puts two unbounded regions on top of each other in
    the first place: tabs, a capped secondary list, a master-detail split. Engineering cannot get
    there alone, because "don't stack these" is a design decision; this is
    <a href='#shared-goal'>§12.1's channel</a> paying for itself. When the conversation happens at mock
    time, the answer is a better composition; when it happens at build time, the answer is a partition.
  </p>

  <h2 id='hard-patterns'>12.4 Other patterns that fight containment</h2>
  <p>
    Stacked unbounded regions are the most common offender, but the same analysis (walk the states,
    find who moves) catches a family of patterns worth recognizing on sight, in review, before they
    are built:
  </p>
  <table class='tbl'>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Pattern</th>
        <th>Why it shifts</th>
        <th>Stable version</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Stacked unbounded lists/tables</strong></td>
        <td>Regions of unknown size compete for one axis; whoever loads first wins.</td>
        <td>Partitioned panes with internal scroll; or cap one region.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Wizard dialogs sized per step</strong></td>
        <td>
          Each frame has drastically different content, so the dialog resizes (and re-centers!) on
          every step: the world moves between every click.
        </td>
        <td>
          One frame sized for the largest step (smaller steps center inside it), or a top-anchored
          dialog that only ever grows downward.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Banners/alerts injected above content</strong></td>
        <td>
          Appears after first paint and pushes the entire page down exactly when the user starts
          reading.
        </td>
        <td>Reserved banner slot in the chrome, or overlay/toast that floats.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Images and embeds without dimensions</strong></td>
        <td>Height is unknown until the asset loads; every image is a small earthquake.</td>
        <td>
          Aspect-ratio boxes reserved in CSS (<code>next/image</code> requires dimensions for exactly
          this reason).
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Accordions/rows that expand inline</strong></td>
        <td> Expanding one row relocates every row after it; collapse and the page snaps back. </td>
        <td>
          Fine when user-initiated (expected motion); unstable when expanded state is data-driven on
          load. Never auto-expand after paint.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Type-dependent detail panels</strong></td>
        <td>
          A panel that renders wildly different content by record type changes size when the record
          loads.
        </td>
        <td>One panel frame with a designed layout per type, all sharing the frame.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The wizard case deserves the spelled-out version because it hides so well in a comp. Each step
    is mocked as its own artboard, each artboard is individually beautiful, and no artboard shows
    the <em>transition</em>: a short confirmation step does not need the space the split-pane review
    step needs, so the dialog snaps from 720px wide to 400px and back, re-centering each time, and
    the user's eyes chase the buttons around the screen. The stable versions cost one design
    decision: size the frame once for the largest step and let smaller steps breathe inside it, or
    anchor the top edge so growth only ever extends downward. Both are decisions about the <em
      >set</em
    > of steps, which is precisely what a per-artboard mock never forces anyone to make.
  </p>

