  <h3 id='wizard-shell'>Building the one frame: wizard drawers and dialogs</h3>
  <p>
    The design rule above has an engineering twin: <strong>one component per flow.</strong>
    <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='very' /> The drawer or dialog's shell (its container, header, tab bar, footer) is static
    across every step, and only the inner content region changes as the user progresses. Never unmount
    and remount three different drawers as the step changes; that rebuilds, re-lays out, and re-animates
    the exact structure the user is mid-flow inside of. The goal here is the same as everywhere else in
    the UI: <strong
      >hold constant what is static and unchanging, maximize that structure, and minimize the
      dynamic.</strong
    > The one component that <em>is</em>
    mounted and unmounted is the drawer itself: it's a floating element, so it lives in its own dynamically
    imported file, mounted on open and unmounted on close (<a
      href='08-bundle-size-code-splitting.html#triggers'>§8.4</a
    >). Everything below applies verbatim to dialogs; drawers just make the vertical geometry more
    interesting.
  </p>

  <h4 id='wizard-shell-bad'>The wrong shape: a drawer per step</h4>
  <p>
    The failure mode is worth seeing before the fix. Give each step its own drawer component and the
    flow becomes a parade of teardowns: React sees a different element type on every advance, so it
    unmounts the entire tree the user is standing in and mounts a lookalike.
  </p>

