    <Fragment slot='caption'>
      The tabbed anatomy: the same two double-headed arrows (the drawer fills the viewport height and
      disallows overflow; the static blue content region reserves and fills the space below the tab
      bar), with the active tab shown as a darker filled tile rather than an underline. The user skips
      around freely, and just as in the sequential case, either kind of content slots into the same
      fixed region: only the amber inner frame ever changes.
    </Fragment>
  </Diagram>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">ProgramDetailsDrawer.tsx - non-sequential steps: static tabs, same doctrine</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">const</span> TABS = { <span class="tok-prop">details</span>: DetailsTab, <span class="tok-prop">schedule</span>: ScheduleTab, <span class="tok-prop">history</span>: HistoryTab } <span class="tok-kw">as const</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramDetailsDrawer</span>({ programId, onClose }: <span class="tok-fn">Props</span>) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [tab, setTab] = <span class="tok-fn">useState</span>&lt;<span class="tok-kw">keyof typeof</span> TABS&gt;(<span class="tok-str">"details"</span>);
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> Tab = TABS[tab];

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Drawer</span> <span class="tok-prop">onClose</span>={onClose}&gt;
      &lt;div <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"flex max-h-full grow flex-col overflow-hidden"</span>&gt;
        &lt;<span class="tok-fn">DrawerHeader</span> <span class="tok-prop">title</span>=<span class="tok-str">"Program details"</span> <span class="tok-prop">onClose</span>={onClose} /&gt;

        <span class="tok-com">{/* Static: the tab bar holds still; the user skips around freely. */}</span>
        &lt;<span class="tok-fn">TabList</span> <span class="tok-prop">value</span>={tab} <span class="tok-prop">onChange</span>={setTab} /&gt;

        <span class="tok-com">{/* Static container: everything below the tabs, stretched to the
            bottom. Only the tab panel INSIDE it swaps. */}</span>
        &lt;div <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"flex min-h-0 grow flex-col overflow-hidden"</span>&gt;
          &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Tab</span> <span class="tok-prop">programId</span>={programId} /&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;
      &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Drawer</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      The tabbed cousin: no footer, tabs pinned at the top, and the same flex-grow region absorbing
      everything beneath them. Tab identity in <code>useState</code> is correct here, not a violation
      of <a href='09-server-resolvable-state.html#tabs'>§9.3</a>: these tabs live inside a transient
      overlay, not a page, which is exactly the ladder's bottom rung. One drawer, three tabs, and
      only <code>TABS[tab]</code> ever changes.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <h2 id='seed-data'>12.5 Seed data is part of the discipline</h2>
  <p>
    There is a development-side habit that keeps all of the above honest:
    <strong>test with data volumes that vary wildly.</strong>
    <Chip k='very' /> A dev database seeded with three tidy rows per table is the engineering version
    of the happy-path comp: every layout looks contained because nothing ever has enough content to fight.
    The failure modes in this chapter only become visible when the seed data forces them: one table with
    1 row above a table with 50; a program title that wraps to three lines; a list that is legitimately
    empty; a page where one region resolves instantly and its sibling takes two seconds.
  </p>
  <p>
    So seed generously and unevenly, on purpose: some entities with 0 children, some with 1, some
    with 5, some with 50 or 500. Make the skew part of the fixture set rather than something you
    construct by hand once and lose. And when a layout survives the skewed seed <em>and</em> a throttled
    network, it has actually earned the word "stable": the states design signed off on and the states
    the seed data forces are, together, the whole state space.
  </p>

  <Callout kind='info' title='The checklist, for design reviews'>
    <p>
      For every data-driven region in the mock: (1) What renders before the data? (2) What does 0 /
      1 / 50 items look like? (3) When it's bigger than its space: grow, scroll, paginate, or
      truncate? (4) Which regions arrive at different times, and does the page look intentional in
      both orders? (5) Does anything already painted ever move? If a question has no answer, the
      mock isn't done; if the answer is "it moves," the design isn't stable.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id='rules'>The rules</h2>
  <table class='tbl'>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Rule</th>
        <th>Scope</th>
        <th>Importance</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>Keep the engineering/UX channel open, in both directions:</strong>
          containment concerns surface while a design is still a design, not after the layout is built.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='ux' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Every data-driven region in a mock needs its <strong
            >pre-data, empty, sparse, and overflowing states</strong
          > answered before it is approved.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='ux' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>Regions of unknown size must not compete for one axis.</strong> Partition the space,
          cap one region, or change the composition.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='ux' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Overlays and dialogs get <strong>one frame per flow</strong>, sized for the largest step;
          steps change inside the frame.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='ux' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='very' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Seed data <strong>skewed on purpose</strong> (0 / 1 / 5 / 50 / 500) so unstable layouts fail
          in development, not in front of users.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='ux' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='very' /></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

